    <section xml:id="sec-accounting-variability-r">
        <title>Accounting for Variability in R</title>

        <insight xml:id="insight-boxplot-variability-types">
            # this goes into Ch10 I think
            <title>Two kinds of variability in one chart</title>

            <p>
                The chart in <xref ref="fig-ch9-africa-boxplot-years"/> shows
                two distinct kinds of variability at the same time, and it is
                worth separating them.
            </p>

            <p>
                The first is
                <term>variability across countries within a single 
  year</term>,
                shown by the height of each box.
                In any given year, some African countries have life expectancies
                well above the median and some well below.
                What drives this?
                Some of it could in principle be accounted for — if we had data
                on each country's wealth, access to healthcare, sanitation
                infrastructure, or disease burden, we could account for some of
                why one country sits above the box and another sits below it.
                The gapminder dataset gives us GDP per capita as a rough proxy
                for some of those things, but not all.
                Some drivers are harder to capture in data at all, and would
                hence be unaccounted for: governance quality, geography, the
                strength of public health systems, the legacy of particular
                conflicts.
                And some of the remaining variation between countries is simply
                natural: even two countries that look identical on every
                variable we can measure will not have exactly the same life
                expectancy.
            </p>

            <p>
                The second is
                <term>variability across years within Kenya</term>, illustrated
                by the rise and fall of the teal line.
                Kenya's life expectancy in 1952 was around 42 years; by 1987 it
                had risen to nearly 60; by 2002 it had fallen back to around 51,
                before recovering again.
                Again, some of it could in principle be accounted for.
                If we had data on improvements in medicine, sanitation, and
                nutrition over time, or on the spread of the HIV/AIDS epidemic,
                we could account for much of the rise and the subsequent dip.
                But we do not have those variables here.
                Some drivers are harder to capture in data at all: droughts,
                political instability, specific health policy decisions in
                particular years.
                These all affected Kenyan life expectancy but are rarely
                available as measured variables.
                And some is natural: even in a stable period, life expectancy
                fluctuates slightly from year to year for reasons no dataset
                would ever fully capture.
            </p>

            <p>
                You might notice that year is in our dataset, and we can see
                that life expectancy in Kenya changes depending on year.
                So does year account for the variability in Kenya's life
                expectancy over time?
                In a narrow sense, yes: if you know the year, you can predict
                roughly where Kenya's life expectancy will sit.
                But there is an important distinction between
                <term>accounting for</term> variability and
                <term>explaining</term> it.
                Year accounts for the pattern — it tells you when things
                changed.
                It does not explain why.
                The actual causes of the rise (improvements in medicine,
                sanitation, and nutrition) and the dip (the HIV/AIDS epidemic)
                are not in this dataset.
                Year is acting as a proxy for all of them combined.
                Accounting for variability means being able to predict or
                describe a pattern; explaining it means knowing what actually
                drove it.
                The two are not the same, and confusing them is one of the most
                common mistakes in data analysis.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
            fig-ch9-least-squares-comparison -- The reduction in square area
            from left to right is the reduction in unaccounted-for variability.
        </p>
    </section>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-ch10">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-variability-types">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For each of the following situations, identify which type of
                    variability is being described (accounted-for,
                    unaccounted-for, or natural) and briefly explain your
                    reasoning.
                </p>

                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Matatu journey times in Nairobi are consistently
                            longer on Friday evenings than on Tuesday mornings.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Two students take the same boda-boda route on the
                            same Tuesday morning and arrive at slightly
                            different times, even though nothing unusual
                            happened on either journey.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Life expectancy in several southern African
                            countries dropped sharply in the 1990s due to the
                            HIV/AIDS epidemic, but this variable was not
                            included in the dataset being analysed.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            A farmer's crop yield varies from year to year.
                            The analyst has data on rainfall but not on soil
                            quality, which also affects yield.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</chapter>